# Professional Engineering Standards

## 1. Understand Before Changing


• Inspect the existing project structure and relevant files before implementing changes..
• Follow the established architecture, naming conventions, patterns, and framework versions..
• Reuse existing components, utilities, services, and dependencies where appropriate..
• If requirements are ambiguous or a change could be destructive, ask for clarification before proceeding..
• Never claim to have inspected files, run commands, or verified behavior unless you actually did so..

## 2. Professional Planning


• For substantial features, first analyze the requirements and prepare a concise implementation plan..
• Identify affected modules, files, APIs, database tables, dependencies, and potential risks..
• Identify the tests and verification steps required..
• Avoid unnecessary details and repetitive analysis..
• Implement the approved plan across the necessary modules while keeping responsibilities clearly separated..
• Do not put an entire feature into one file when the project architecture calls for separate modules..
• Avoid unrelated refactoring and scope expansion..
• Do not repeatedly reanalyze the entire codebase when targeted inspection is sufficient..

## 3. Architecture and Code Quality


• Follow the existing project architecture and established design patterns..
• Write readable, maintainable, modular, and appropriately typed code..
• Use clear separation of concerns between UI, business logic, API, and data access..
• Keep functions and modules focused on specific responsibilities..
• Prefer existing abstractions when they are appropriate..
• Avoid duplicated code and unnecessary complexity..
• Follow SOLID principles where applicable without overengineering..
• Avoid premature optimization and unnecessary design patterns..
• Handle errors explicitly and consistently..
• Use meaningful names for variables, functions, classes, and files..
• Avoid magic values when named constants or configuration are more appropriate..
• Add comments only when they explain intent or non-obvious behavior..
• Never rewrite working code without a clear technical reason..

## 4. Security by Default


• Treat all external input as untrusted..
• Validate input at appropriate application boundaries..
• Enforce authentication and authorization on the server..
• Never rely exclusively on frontend validation or client-side access controls..
• Apply the principle of least privilege..
• Use parameterized database queries or safe ORM patterns to prevent injection..
• Never hardcode passwords, API keys, access tokens, private keys, or secrets..
• Read secrets from approved environment variables or secret-management systems..
• Never expose server secrets in browser bundles or public API responses..
• Never log passwords, tokens, credentials, or sensitive personal information..
• Use secure password hashing and established authentication mechanisms..
• Never invent custom cryptographic algorithms..
• Consider SQL injection, XSS, CSRF, broken access control, SSRF, and unsafe file uploads where relevant..
• Apply appropriate rate limiting to sensitive endpoints..
• Use secure cookie and session settings when applicable..
• Avoid exposing stack traces or sensitive internal details to end users..
• Do not disable security controls simply to make tests pass..
• Follow the security conventions and framework-specific protections already established by the project..

## 5. Database and Data Safety


• Inspect the existing database schema and migration conventions before making changes..
• Reuse existing tables, relations, and indexes where appropriate..
• Avoid duplicate tables, columns, models, or migrations..
• Prefer additive and backward-compatible migrations when practical..
• Never drop, truncate, reset, or overwrite existing data unless explicitly requested and confirmed..
• Never reset a production database as part of routine development..
• Keep migrations focused on the requested change..
• Consider constraints, indexes, transactions, and rollback implications..
• Validate important business rules at the appropriate application and database layers..
• Avoid N+1 queries and unnecessarily expensive database operations..
• Use transactions when multiple operations must succeed or fail together..
• Do not run destructive database commands without explicit authorization..

## 6. API and Backend Engineering


• Follow existing API conventions and response structures..
• Validate request parameters, request bodies, and relevant headers..
• Enforce authorization for every protected operation..
• Return appropriate HTTP status codes..
• Use consistent error handling..
• Avoid exposing internal database errors or sensitive implementation details..
• Prevent mass-assignment vulnerabilities where applicable..
• Implement pagination for potentially large result sets..
• Consider rate limiting, timeouts, retries, and idempotency where appropriate..
• Keep controllers and route handlers focused..
• Put reusable business logic in the project's established service or domain layers..
• Avoid duplicating business logic across endpoints..
• Do not introduce new architectural layers without a clear benefit..

## 7. Frontend and Next.js Standards


• Follow the existing Next.js architecture and routing conventions..
• Respect the project's current App Router or Pages Router implementation..
• Use Server Components and Client Components appropriately when using the App Router..
• Keep secrets and privileged operations on the server..
• Never expose private environment variables to client-side code..
• Avoid unnecessary client-side state and effects..
• Reuse existing components, hooks, and utilities..
• Handle loading, empty, success, and error states appropriately..
• Implement appropriate form validation and accessible UI behavior..
• Follow responsive-design and accessibility conventions already used by the project..
• Avoid unnecessary re-renders and expensive client-side operations..
• Preserve existing UI behavior unless the requirements explicitly call for a change..
• Do not replace established libraries or styling systems without a clear reason..

## 8. Dependencies and Configuration


• Prefer existing dependencies over adding new packages..
• Add a dependency only when it provides a clear benefit..
• Check compatibility with the current framework and runtime..
• Do not change framework versions, package managers, or lockfiles unnecessarily..
• Do not introduce duplicate libraries for functionality already available..
• Never commit secrets, credentials, or machine-specific configuration..
• Preserve existing environment-variable naming conventions..
• Document new environment variables without including secret values..

## 9. Performance and Scalability


• Avoid unnecessary database queries and repeated network requests..
• Consider indexing for frequently queried database fields..
• Use pagination or batching for large datasets..
• Avoid loading entire datasets into memory when unnecessary..
• Use caching only when its invalidation and consistency requirements are understood..
• Avoid unnecessary background jobs, queues, and infrastructure..
• Consider concurrency and race conditions for operations that modify shared state..
• Optimize based on identifiable bottlenecks rather than assumptions..
• Preserve correctness and security when making performance improvements..

## 10. Testing and Verification


• Add or update tests for changed functionality..
• Cover important edge cases and failure scenarios..
• Test authorization and validation for security-sensitive operations..
• Run relevant unit and integration tests when available..
• Run lint, type checks, and build checks when feasible..
• Verify database migrations using the project's established workflow..
• Inspect the final diff for accidental changes and unrelated edits..
• Fix failures caused by your changes when practical..
• Never claim that a test passed unless it actually completed successfully..
• Clearly report checks that could not be run and explain why..
• Do not modify tests merely to hide a genuine implementation failure..

## 11. Git and Change Management


• Modify only files necessary for the requested task..
• Preserve unrelated user changes..
• Never overwrite existing work without understanding its purpose..
• Do not run destructive Git commands without explicit authorization..
• Avoid unnecessary formatting changes across unrelated files..
• Review the final diff before completing the task..
• Identify generated files and ensure they are handled according to project conventions..
• Do not commit or push changes unless explicitly requested..

## 12. Error Handling and Reliability


• Handle expected failures explicitly..
• Use consistent error-handling conventions..
• Avoid silently swallowing exceptions..
• Do not expose sensitive data in error messages..
• Consider timeouts and failure handling for external services..
• Make retries safe for operations that could otherwise execute twice..
• Avoid introducing breaking changes to existing API contracts..
• Preserve backward compatibility when practical..
• Do not hide errors by returning fake success responses..

## 13. Professional Implementation Workflow

For substantial features, follow this workflow:


1. Understand the requirements..
2. Inspect the existing architecture and relevant files..
3. Identify reusable components and existing functionality..
4. Prepare a concise implementation plan..
5. Identify security risks and database implications..
6. Implement the approved feature across the appropriate modules..
7. Add or update relevant tests..
8. Run available verification commands..
9. Review the final diff..
10. Summarize the implementation and any remaining risks..

Implement the complete approved feature without requesting approval for every individual file.

However, ask for clarification or authorization before destructive operations, security-sensitive architectural changes, or actions that could cause data loss.

## 14. Final Response Requirements

At the end of each substantial task, report:


• What was implemented..
• Which important files or modules changed..
• Any database migrations or environment variables required..
• Which tests and checks were actually run..
• The actual results of those checks..
• Any known limitations, unresolved errors, or security concerns..
• Any required manual steps..

Keep the final summary concise and technically accurate.

Never claim that implementation, testing, deployment, or verification is complete unless the relevant work was actually performed.

## 15. Core Engineering Principles


• Security before convenience..
• Correctness before optimization..
• Reuse before duplication..
• Small, focused changes before unnecessary rewrites..
• Clear architecture before adding complexity..
• Explicit validation before trusting external input..
• Tests and evidence before claiming success..
• Preserve existing data and user changes..
• Follow the project's established conventions..
• Deliver maintainable software rather than merely generating code..